proof:
For (a), replacing x by pi/2-x preserves |sin x|+|cos x| and preserves sin(2x), since sin(pi-2x)=sin(2x). Replacing x by pi+x preserves both quantities. Applying the first equality to pi+x gives f(3pi/2-x)=f(pi+x)=f(x).

For (b), put s=sqrt(2). On a quadrant where sin(2x)>=0, let y=|sin x|+|cos x|. Then y in [1,s] and sin(2x)=y^2-1. Thus f(x)=0 is equivalent to P(y)=3y^2-ay+4=0. On a quadrant where sin(2x)<=0, the same y satisfies sin(2x)=1-y^2, so f(x)=0 is equivalent to Q(y)=3y^2+ay-10=0.

For y in [1,s], P(y)=0 is equivalent to a=A(y)=3y+4/y. The function A has its minimum 4sqrt(3) at y=2/sqrt(3), and A(1)=7, A(s)=5sqrt(2). Hence the numbers of roots of P in the open interval (1,s) are respectively 0, 1, 2, 1, 0 when a lies in (-infinity,4sqrt(3)), {4sqrt(3)}, (4sqrt(3),7), [7,5sqrt(2)), and (5sqrt(2),infinity), with the endpoint cases interpreted separately: at a=7, y=1 is a root and y=4/3 is the sole root in (1,s); at a=5sqrt(2), y=s is a root and there is no root in (1,s).

Also, Q(y)=0 is equivalent to a=B(y)=10/y-3y. Since B is strictly decreasing on [1,s], with B(1)=7 and B(s)=2sqrt(2), Q has one root in (1,s) exactly when 2sqrt(2)<a<7. At a=2sqrt(2) its root is y=s, and at a=7 its root is y=1.

Within a half-quadrant of a fixed sign of sin(2x), each y in (1,s) occurs at exactly two points, while y=s occurs at exactly one midpoint. The value y=1 occurs at the quadrant endpoints. Therefore, in (0,npi), the root counts are:
- 0 if a<2sqrt(2) or a>5sqrt(2);
- n if a=2sqrt(2) or a=5sqrt(2);
- 2n if 2sqrt(2)<a<4sqrt(3) or 7<a<5sqrt(2);
- 4n if a=4sqrt(3);
- 6n if 4sqrt(3)<a<7;
- 4n-1 if a=7.
For a=7, the last count is 2n roots from the unique interior P-root in each positive-sine half-period, plus 2n-1 roots with y=1 at x=kpi/2 for 1<=k<=2n-1.

Equating these counts to 2007 gives n=2007 in the two cases with count n, and 4n-1=2007 gives n=502. The counts 2n, 4n, and 6n cannot equal the odd number 2007. This proves exactly the stated three pairs.
